# Usage & Configuration

## Goals

| Goal | Description | Full Reference |
|---|---|---|
| `ref-count:count-references` | Analyzes a single project module's compiled bytecode against its dependencies. | [count-references](count-references-mojo.html) |
| `ref-count:aggregate` | Aggregates dependency reference statistics across all non-`pom` modules in a multi-module reactor. | [aggregate](aggregate-mojo.html) |
| `ref-count:help` | Displays help and parameter details on the command line (`-Ddetail=true`). | [help](help-mojo.html) |

## Zero-Config CLI Execution

To try the plugin on any Maven project without modifying its `pom.xml`:

```bash
mvn compile io.github.jona5:ref-count-maven-plugin:${project.version}:count-references
```

To also inspect test classes and `test`-scoped dependencies, run `test-compile` first:

```bash
mvn test-compile io.github.jona5:ref-count-maven-plugin:${project.version}:count-references \
  -Drefcount.includeTestClasses=true
```

## Configuring in `pom.xml`

Add the plugin to `<build><plugins>` and optionally bind it to the `verify` lifecycle phase:

```xml
<plugin>
    <groupId>io.github.jona5</groupId>
    <artifactId>ref-count-maven-plugin</artifactId>
    <version>${project.version}</version>
    <configuration>
        <showUnused>true</showUnused>
        <includeTransitive>true</includeTransitive>
        <lowUsageThreshold>3</lowUsageThreshold>
        <excludes>
            <exclude>org.slf4j:*</exclude>
        </excludes>
    </configuration>
    <executions>
        <execution>
            <id>count-refs</id>
            <phase>verify</phase>
            <goals>
                <goal>count-references</goal>
            </goals>
        </execution>
    </executions>
</plugin>
```

## Reading the Output

```text
[INFO] --- Reference Analysis Results ---
[INFO]   org.apache.commons:commons-lang3:3.12.0 -> 42 references [direct, 574 KB, 4 dep classes in 12 project classes]
[INFO]   com.google.guava:guava:33.0-jre -> 2 references [direct, 3.0 MB, 1 dep class in 1 project class, low usage]
[INFO]       com.example.App -> com.google.common.collect.Lists
[INFO]   org.example:transitive-lib:2.1.0 -> 3 references [transitive - undeclared!, 92 KB, 1 dep class in 2 project classes]
[INFO]   org.example:unused-lib:1.0.0 -> 0 references [direct, 48 KB]
[INFO] -------------------------------------
```

- **Reference count**: Total bytecode references found across method calls, field accesses, type instructions, arrays, inheritance, generic signatures, annotations, `try/catch` blocks, class literals, lambdas, and method references.
- **`direct` vs. `transitive - undeclared!`**: Distinguishes directly declared dependencies from transitive dependencies your code references directly.
- **`X dep classes in Y project classes`**: Shows *surface area* (how many classes of the dependency are used) and *blast radius* (how many project classes rely on it).
- **`low usage`**: Automatically flags lightly used dependencies (`0 < references <= lowUsageThreshold`) and prints the exact class-to-class mapping.

## Multi-Module Aggregation

Run `ref-count:aggregate` from the root of a multi-module project after compiling all modules:

```bash
mvn compile ref-count:aggregate
```

The report consolidates reference counts across all reactor modules and reports how many modules depend on each artifact.

## Exporting Reports (JSON, CSV, Markdown)

Set `outputFormat` to `json`, `csv`, or `markdown` (`md`). By default, reports are written to `target/ref-count-report.{json,csv,md}` while still logging the console summary:

```bash
mvn compile ref-count:count-references -Drefcount.outputFormat=json
mvn compile ref-count:count-references -Drefcount.outputFormat=csv
```

### GitHub Actions Step Summary

Write a Markdown table directly to the GitHub Actions job summary:

```yaml
- name: Dependency Reference Count
  run: |
    ./mvnw -B compile ref-count:count-references \
      -Drefcount.outputFormat=markdown \
      -Drefcount.outputFile="$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY" \
      -Drefcount.lowUsageThreshold=3
```

## Migration Ratchet (Baseline Mode)

When migrating away from a dependency over multiple pull requests, freeze the current reference count in a baseline file:

```bash
mvn compile ref-count:count-references -Drefcount.updateBaseline=true
```

This writes a sorted, version-agnostic `ref-count-baseline.json` (keyed by `groupId:artifactId`, so dependency version updates do not break the baseline). You can either track all dependencies or keep only the libraries being migrated:

```json
{
  "com.google.guava:guava": 15
}
```

Enforce the baseline in CI:

```bash
mvn compile ref-count:count-references -Drefcount.baselineFile=ref-count-baseline.json
```

If any tracked dependency's reference count increases above the baseline, the build fails (configurable via `failOnIncrease=false` to warn only). Improvements are logged automatically.

For the complete list of parameters, defaults, and Maven properties, see the auto-generated [count-references](count-references-mojo.html) and [aggregate](aggregate-mojo.html) goal documentation.
